; 38-bindop.kurt

; `bindop` marks a symbol as a *variable-binding operator*: its first
; argument must be a variable (or a boolean expression whose first part is a
; variable, e.g. `forall x>0 F(x)`) that is then considered bound inside the
; rest of the expression.  `forall` and `exists` (used already in lessons
; 21-let.kurt and 22-pick.kurt) are declared exactly this way, in
; `logic.kurt`:
;
;     arity  forall 2
;     bindop forall
;
; A symbol needs an `arity` (37-arity.kurt) before it can be declared a
; `bindop`.  Here is a minimal binder of our own:

arity myforall 2
bindop myforall

bool P
const P
arity P 1

parse myforall x (P x)   ; (myforall x (P x)) -- `x` is bound in `(P x)`

; As with the other syntax keywords, `bindop` only sets up grammar (and the
; check that the first argument really is an unbound variable) -- the actual
; reasoning rules ("forall-elim" etc.) are ordinary `use` axioms, exactly
; like the ones in `logic.kurt`.
